### 4.5 Outlook

If the beam luminosity is high enough -- and the event rate higher than the DAQ storage rate -- the efficient \(Z=-2\) trigger of L3 is able to enhance the \({}^{3}\overline{\rm He}\) signal and thus to provide a high statistics event sample for the offline analysis. At the same time, L3 brings the observation of those exotic clusters with \(Z=-2\) within the reach of the STAR, for which a very large data set is needed, i.e. 10M central events or more. First, the detection of anti-hypertritons \(\frac{3}{\Lambda}\overline{\rm H}\), decaying partially into \({}^{3}\overline{\rm He}+\pi^{-}\), and second, the observation of the anti-alpha \({}^{4}\overline{\rm He}\). Both would be observed for the first time. The event sample collected so far could be sufficient for a first hint of the \(\frac{3}{\Lambda}\overline{\rm H}\), whereas for the detection of \({}^{4}\overline{\rm He}\) a factor of 5 or 10 more events need to be scanned. For this large number of events, the L3 trigger becomes essential to reduce the amount of events for storage and the time consuming offline analysis.

